    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1041">

                <pb facs="1220" n="1024" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg1024"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Pauley
                    Files</note>
                <head>No. 1041<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Draft of Report by the Economic
                        Subcommittee</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1041-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1041-f2"/>]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-sibling-dates" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1945-07-21T23:00:00+01:00"
                            notBefore="1945-07-18T00:00:00-04:00">Undated</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Draft Report of the Economic
                        Sub-Committee</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">A. <hi rendition="#italic">Economic Principles</hi></p>
                <p>The U. S. and U. K. representatives have agreed to
                    paragraph 13 (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1041-f3"/>
                    of the Economic Principles only on the assumption that “Germany” is Germany as
                    it existed on the 31st December, 1937. On this assumption they maintain that the
                    principle should be accepted that, in the absence of special reasons to the
                    contrary, the food and fuel requirements of the Greater Berlin area should be
                    drawn from those areas in Germany from which they were derived before the war. On the other hand, if any part of Germany as
                    it existed on the 31st December, 1937 is to be administered by, or transferred
                    to, Poland, suitable arrangements should be made for the rest of Germany,
                    including the Greater Berlin area, to have a prior claim to the surplus food and
                    coal produced in such transferred territory to meet deficiencies in local
                    supplies. This arrangement should last at least for so long as the extreme
                    shortage of coal and foodstuffs <pb facs="1221" n="1025" xml:id="pg1025"/>
                    continues. The Soviet representatives do not accept this principle and maintain
                    that, whatever the frontiers of Germany may be, equitable distribution of
                    essential commodities between zones should take place.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
